80 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 200 0 R
/E (Consists of two virtual interfaces, Tap0 and Tap1, which can communicate with each other \(for example, using the ping command\).)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
81 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 201 0 R
/E (In between them, there is a wrapper that acts as a bridge. It reads the raw Ethernet frames from the TAP interfaces and injects them directly into the input pins of the top.sv circuit. At the same time, it records a .fst file for waveform visualization.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
82 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 202 0 R
/E (The digital circuit top.sv implements the hardware PHY layer. It instantiates the MAC and connects it via the XGMII bus \(64-bit data + 8-bit control\) to the PCS 64b/66b Encoder/Decoder and the 58-bit LFSR Scrambler.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
83 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 203 0 R
/E (In this case, Lab 3, the data inside the hardware is processed by a MAC layer, then XGMII, and encoded into 66-bit blocks by a PCS 64b/66b module, and converted into pseudo-random noise by a 58-bit LFSR Scrambler before being decoded at the receiver.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
84 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 204 0 R
/E (Finally, the packets are analyzed using Wireshark and GTKWave.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
